{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>You shall not take the name of YHWH your Elohim in vain.</b> It would have been fitting to say 'You shall not take My name,' for it was HaShem who was speaking until now." "<b>And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote:</b> This validates the tradition of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, who said: 'I am' and 'You shall have no other gods' — they heard from the mouth of the Almighty. For the Holy One, blessed be He, desired that they hear these two from His mouth, so that all Israel would be prophets in the faith of the existence of YHWH and His unity, and in the prohibition of idolatry. Therefore He spoke until now in the language of the blessed Name, but now, beginning with the utterance 'You shall not take,' He said 'the name of YHWH your Elohim.' And the reason this commandment of 'You shall not take [the name]' was arranged after the warning against idolatry is that just as it is fitting to fear the great and awesome Name so as not to give His honor to another, so it is fitting to give honor to His name; and one who bears it in vain profanes it, in the manner of what is written (Leviticus 19:12): ״ולא תשבעו בשמי לשקר וחללת את שם אלהיך״ — 'And you shall not swear by My name falsely, and you shall profane the name of your Elohim.' And this verse warns that a person should not swear by the name for naught, such as one who swears about something known, to alter it or to confirm it — that is, one who swears about a pillar of gold that it is of marble or of gold. And He used the expression 'You shall not take (לא תשא)' and did not say 'You shall not swear (לא תשבע),' because it further includes that a person should not bear the name on his lips for nothing, in the language of (Exodus 23:1): ״לא תשא שמע שוא״, and (Psalms 16:4): ״ובל אשא את שמותם על שפתי״ — for speech is called thus because one lifts up (נושא) the voice in it, and this is called in the language of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, 'taking the name of Heaven in vain.' And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: From where do we learn that a person should not say, 'To HaShem an burnt-offering, to HaShem a sin-offering,' but rather 'A burnt-offering to HaShem, a sin-offering to HaShem, an offering to HaShem'? And is this not an a fortiori argument — if for one who is about to dedicate, the Torah said that My name shall not apply except to the offering, then a fortiori for one who takes the name of Heaven in vain — end quote. And the reason for the prohibition of swearing is that it denies the truth from its very root and destroys faith from his mouth; and whether the oath be by taking hold of an object or without taking hold of an object, he is like one who denies the Torah or its Giver, since he nullified His words and transgressed His oath. And thus R. Eliezer, of blessed memory, wrote: One who transgresses an oath is as one who denies and disbelieves in HaShem, may He be exalted, for the intent of the oath is: just as HaShem is true, so shall my word be true; and if he does not fulfill his word, this is as one who denies HaShem, may He be exalted. And the custom of the people of Egypt to this day: if one swears by the life of the king and does not fulfill his word, he is liable to death, and even if he gave a ransom of his weight in gold, he shall not live, for he has despised the king publicly; if so, for a king of flesh and blood, whose beginning is vanity and whose kingship is vanity and whose end is vanity, then a fortiori, a thousand thousand times, a person is obligated to be careful in his oath to HaShem, may He be exalted." "<b>And now:</b> We have seen that because the children of Israel swore in public concerning the matter of the concubine in Gibeah — and Phinehas the high priest was there — we find that those who transgressed the oath were killed, namely the people of Jabesh-gilead, men, women, and children; and this is not done even to Sabbath desecrators. And we have also seen that Saul sought to kill Jonathan his son, for he did not hear the oath, as it says (I Samuel 14:27): ״וכיונתן לא שמע בהשביע אביו את העם״ — 'But Jonathan had not heard when his father adjured the people.' And the power of the oath was very great, for Jonathan did not transgress willfully but unintentionally, and even so his father made him liable to death, until the people had to ransom him, as it says (ibid.): ״ויפדו העם את יונתן ולא מת״ — 'And the people ransomed Jonathan and he did not die.' And they ransomed him according to law, for they said it was unintentional. From here one may learn that one who transgresses an oath is liable to death like those liable to death by the court. Come and see: for Scripture equated one who transgresses an oath to the prohibition of another man's wife — it is written here: ״לא ינקה״, and it is written there (Proverbs 6:29): ״כן הבא אל אשת רעהו לא ינקה כל הנוגע בה״. And we saw that the Holy One, blessed be He, brought famine upon the land because of Saul and his house, who transgressed the oath of the princes who had sworn to the Gibeonites; and when the men of blood were killed, then (II Samuel 21:14): ״ויעתר אלהים לארץ״. And Solomon said to Shimei (I Kings 2:42): ״הלא השבעתיך בה'״. And the principle: we do not find in the Ten Commandments an explicit reward except for honoring father and mother, and no explicit punishment except for idolatry and for a vain oath. And many think that one who takes the name of HaShem in vain has not committed a great transgression, but I will show him that it is more severe than all the prohibitions that follow it. For the murderer and the adulterer — who are grave transgressors — cannot murder or commit adultery at every moment they wish, for they will be afraid; but one who has accustomed himself to swear in vain will swear in a single day countless oaths without number, and from the force of habit, the oath will precede the speech, and it is for them a figure of speech. And even if there were in Israel only this one transgression, it would suffice to prolong the exile and to add blow upon blow to our afflictions. And I will show them their madness: the murderer, if he killed his enemy, satisfied his desire and his vengeance; and so the adulterer for his moment, and the thief for his enjoyment at the time, until the lie catches up with the murder or the vengeance; but one who swears falsely profanes HaShem publicly without any benefit to himself — end quote." "<b>And we find:</b> Isaiah the prophet reproves Israel for this transgression, as it is written (Isaiah 48:1): ״שמעו זאת בית יעקב הנקראים בשם ישראל וממי יהודה יצאו הנשבעים בשם ה' ובאלהי ישראל יזכירו לא באמת ולא בצדקה כי מעיר הקדש נקראו ועל אלהי ישראל נסמכו ה' צבאות שמו״ — 'Hear this, O house of Jacob, who are called by the name of Israel, and from the waters of Judah they came forth, who swear by the name of YHWH and make mention of the Elohim of Israel — not in truth and not in righteousness; for they are called from the holy city, and they lean upon the Elohim of Israel; YHWH of hosts is His name.' Isaiah, peace be upon him, intended in these two verses to say that one should not marvel at the gentiles if they stumble in this sin, for they did not receive the Torah and did not see the light and do not know and do not understand — in darkness they walk; but the wonder is about those who are the house of Jacob and are called by the name of Israel, who received the Torah and heard the utterance 'You shall not take.' Therefore he mentioned 'the house of Jacob,' in the language said here at the giving of the Torah (Exodus 19:3): ״כה תאמר לבית יעקב ותגיד לבני ישראל״ — 'So shall you say to the house of Jacob and tell the children of Israel' — for at the giving of the Torah the name Israel was established for them, a name derived from dominion (שררה), for they saw the dominion of the Blessed One and His glory face to face, and from this they were called Jeshurun. And he said: ״וממי יהודה יצאו״ — that is, from the seed of Judah. And he mentioned Judah because the beginning of the prophecy of Isaiah was to the tribe of Judah, as it is written (Isaiah 1:1): ״אשר חזה על יהודה וירושלים״." "<b>And now:</b> The prophet, peace be upon him, in his language expresses astonishment — how is it possible for the house of Jacob, who received the Torah and are called by the name of Israel and came forth from the tribe of Judah, from which come the light and the kingship, that they should swear by the name of YHWH and make mention of the Elohim of Israel and not be careful about the mention of HaShem constantly? And they were called from the holy city, so it behooved them to be holy, just as they were called 'a kingdom of priests and a holy nation.' And ״על אלהי ישראל נסמכו״ — 'they lean upon the Elohim of Israel' — in their mouths, not in their hearts, in the manner of what is written (Jeremiah 12:2–3): ״קרוב אתה בפיהם ורחוק מכליותיהם״ — 'You are near in their mouth and far from their reins.' And he said: ״ה' צבאות שמו״ — 'YHWH of hosts is His name' — for He is the master of all the hosts, all of them, the upper and the lower; therefore you should have feared Him." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> One who transgresses an oath is like one who transgresses a herem (ban), for the oath is the herem and the herem is the oath. How great is the power of the herem? From Achan — for Achan trespassed in the herem, and thirty-six righteous men died on his account, as it says (Joshua 7:5): ״ויכו מהם אנשי העי כשלשים וששה איש״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joshua: Israel has sinned. What did Joshua do? He looked at the twelve stones of the breastplate and saw the stone of Judah dimming its light, and he knew that the tribe of Judah had trespassed in the herem. He cast lots by tribes, from tribes to families, and from families to individual men, and Achan son of Carmi son of Zabdi son of Zerah, of the tribe of Judah, was caught. And so we find with Saul son of Kish, that he imposed a herem in the language of a curse, as it says (I Samuel 14:24): ״ארור האיש אשר יאכל לחם עד הערב״, and it is written: ״והונתן לא שמע בהשביע אביו את העם״ — what Saul imposed in the language of a curse, Scripture calls an oath. And Jonathan ate a little honey, and his eyes brightened. When Saul saw the Philistines gaining strength over Israel, he immediately knew that Israel had trespassed in the herem. He looked at the stones of the breastplate and saw the stone of Benjamin dimming its light, and he knew that the tribe of Benjamin had trespassed in the herem. He cast lots by families, and from families to individual men, and Saul and Jonathan were caught. Saul took his sword to kill Jonathan, as it says (ibid.): ״כה יעשה אלהים וכה יוסיף כי מות תמות יונתן״ — until the people said that it was unwitting, and he brought a sacrifice for his unwitting sin and he was atoned, as it says: ״ויפדו העם את יונתן ולא מת״. And so we find with the brothers of Joseph, that they imposed a herem among themselves that none of them would reveal the matter to Jacob. They said: we are only nine, and a herem is not established except with ten. What did they do? They associated the Holy One, blessed be He, with them, and imposed the herem. Reuben was not present when it happened; when he came, they told him the matter and the herem they had imposed. Reuben heard the herem and remained silent, and did not tell Jacob. And the Holy One, blessed be He, too, because of the herem, remained silent and did not tell Jacob, as it is written (Psalms 147:19): ״מגיד דבריו ליעקב״ — but this matter He did not tell Jacob; rather, on account of the herem He did not tell Jacob. Thus far in the Midrash in Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah.</b> 'You shall not bear the name of YHWH your Elohim in vain' — the word 'name' is said of the Shekhinah, for He will not hold guiltless the unified Name — the one who bears His name in vain. And so it is written (Leviticus 19:12): ״ולא תשבעו בשמי לשקר וחללת את שם אלהיך״ — He means that one who swears by His unified Name falsely profanes the name of his Elohim, for all is unified, this in this. And the severity of the prohibition of the oath is because the word שבועה is derived from שבעה (seven), and they are the seven voices that Moses attained in this holy assembly, and all who transgress the oath testify against themselves that they have no share in the world to come and have departed from the category of the seven. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: What is the difference between oaths and vows? An oath is like swearing by the King Himself; vows are like vowing by the life of the King — the essence of the King. Therefore a vow is more stringent than an oath. A proof of the matter (II Kings 2:2): ״חי ה' וחי נפשך״ — ״חי ה'״, this is the King Himself, and that is the oath; and ״חי נפשך״, this is the life of the King, and that is the vow." "<b>And know</b> that it is not the way of one who swears to give precedence to the weightier and delay the lighter, and therefore Elisha gave precedence to the lighter and delayed the weightier. And for this reason the Torah required a thanksgiving offering for one who transgresses an oath, but for one who transgresses a vow there is no offering at all. And from here you will understand, if you are granted, the secret of the soul and its essence, which is above the oath, for the oath is the King Himself, called Shaddai, and the three — נשמה (soul) — to them, this is (Job 32:8): ״ונשמת שדי תבינם״, for it is from the aspect of Binah, which is Teshuvah. And what is written (Genesis 2:7): ״ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים״ testifies to this, for since the Holy One, blessed be He, breathed into his nostrils, in any case He gave of His spirit within him. And the elucidation of the verse ״ויפח באפיו נשמה״ is that it is drawn from the life of the King, which is the essence of the King and above the King. If so, the essence of the soul of man is from the aspect of Binah, which is Teshuvah; from there is its root, and there it shall return, and this is what is written (Ecclesiastes 12:7): ״והרוח תשוב אל האלהים אשר נתנה״ — He mentioned אלהים, and it is like בראשית ברא אלהים, which is Teshuvah." "<b>And you must know</b> that from there the souls come to Israel by way of the middle line, which is Tiferet; it is the east of the world, for Israel do the will of the Place, as it says (Isaiah 43:5): ״ממזרח אביא זרעך״. And when Israel are evil, they come from the seed that has already come into the world, as they expounded in the Sefer haBahir." "<b>And I have already written</b> of this in the verse ״ויטע״ in the portion of Genesis. And this matter pertains to Israel alone, unlike the nations of the world, who come from there by way of the left, which is the attribute of Gevurah, called the great fire. And therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory said: the slaughter of an idolater is a nevelah and imparts impurity by carrying, because they are from the left side. And because they did not accept the Torah, in which it is written (Deuteronomy 33): ״מימינו אש דת למו״, they are judged by the fire of Gehinnom. And this is the superiority of the souls of Israel over the soul of the nations. And from this the prophet said concerning the merit of Israel (Ezekiel 23): ״וגם את בניהן אשר ילדו לי״ — he explained that Israel are those born to His great Name, for they come by way of 'name.' And one from the nations who converts and accepts the Torah, which was given from within the seven — behold, he is saved from the judgment of Gehinnom, for he enters under the middle line, and from there he returns to his supernal root." "<b>Let us return</b> to our subject and say that one who swears and fulfills his oath receives his reward in the seven mentioned above, and testifies concerning himself that he is the one who testifies and believes in them and longs for them; if he violates his oath, he has departed from their category and is punished in the seven compartments of Gehinnom — for since he did not believe in the seven, he will not merit them, but rather will be punished in seven. And to this secret the masters of truth alluded in their midrash, namely what they said (Song of Songs 2:7): ״השבעתי אתכם בנות ירושלם בצבאות״ — 'I have adjured you, O daughters of Jerusalem, by the hosts': if you merit, you are like the host above, and if not, you are like the host below — end of the midrash. And the explanation of this is that the word השבעתי is as though He said: I place before you these seven or those seven, and permission is in your hands to be as two hosts — either of above or of below. If you merit, you are in the seven firmaments, within those seven called צרור החיים ('the bundle of life'); and if you do not merit, you are as the host below, to be judged in Gehinnom in the seven compartments within it. And this is the explanation of 'if you merit, you are like the host above, and if not, you are like the host below.' May the Holy One, blessed be He, deliver us from punishment and hide us in the shadow of His wings and set our portion with the righteous who trust in Him."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {5 "The citation to Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer is given as ע״כ במדרש בפרקי דר״א, 'thus far in the Midrash in Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer.' Some prooftexts within this passage are cited by book name only (e.g. שם 'ibid.' for I Samuel 14); these are rendered as given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:28.343858948Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}